  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122205,
      "SummaryText": "Colonial Pipeline Company (CPC) \u0096 Greensboro Junction is a petroleum storage facility located in Greensboro, North Carolina. Greensboro Junction includes 74 large aboveground storage tanks containing various grades of gasoline and other petroleum products. Petroleum is received and distributed by pipeline. The Facility also includes a butane operation in which butane is received by trucks, stored on-site in pressurized spheres, and blended into gasoline. This Risk Management Plan (RMP) covers the butane operations conducted at the Facility, identified in this document as the \u0093Butane Blending Process\u0094. The Greensboro Junction Butane Blending Process complies with the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency's Accident Prevention Rules (40 CFR Part 68 Subpart D Program Level 3) as well as the requirements of the U.S. Department of Labor Occupational Safety and Health Administration's Process Safety Management regulations (inclusive of all 14 elements) for Highly Hazardous Chemicals (29 CFR Part 1910.119).\n\nThe Butane Blending Process was constructed in accordance with the National Fire Protection Association Code #58 - The Liquefied Petroleum Gas Code (NFPA 58) and American Petroleum Institute Standard 2510 - Design and Construction of LPG Installations (API 2510). CPC intends to follow the most current future editions to the extent feasible and adhere to all applicable Federal, State, and Local codes/laws. If an emergency were to occur, it is Greensboro Junction's policy to contact the Greensboro Fire Department and the Guilford County LEPC, as appropriate, through the 911 Service and request their assistance. Greensboro Junction maintains a written Emergency Response Action Plan and ensures that it is coordinated with the local response agencies annually at minimum.  The Greensboro Fire Department is aware of the Butane Blending Process, its safety systems, access points, and protective procedures in the event of a significant release.  The facility is fully secured wit"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122205,
      "SummaryText": "h fencing and controlled access in accordance with a written site security plan. In addition, Greensboro Junction currently maintains a trained incipient stage fire response and first responder-operations level hazardous materials response team to respond to the initial stages of an emergency.\n\nAs this document is for a newly constructed process that has not yet begun operations, there have been no accidents or incidents involving the Butane Blending Process that resulted in deaths, injuries, property/environmental damage, evacuations, or sheltering-in-place.\n\nCPC continually evaluates the process for opportunities to improve safety. The Facility completes regular compliance audits to ensure that policies are being implemented properly and revalidates its process hazard analysis every 5 years. In addition CPC actively seeks employee input on process safety improvements."
    }
  ]
